0,"pf":["paper"],"description_full":"Tapestry, Inc. provides accessories and lifestyle brand products in North America, Greater China, rest of Asia, and internationally. The company operates in two segments, Coach and Kate Spade. It offers handbags, fashion designs, business cases, computer bags, messenger style bags, backpacks, travel bags, and totes; accessories, such as small leather goods which includes mini and micro handbags, money pieces, wristlets, pouches, and cosmetic cases, as well as novelty accessories, including address books, time management and travel accessories, sketchbooks, and portfolios; and belts, key rings, technology accessories, gifting, straps, and charms. The company also provides women's and men's footwear, which casual and dress shoes, boots, sneakers, and sandals; and other products which includes outerwear, ready-to-wear, jewelry, watches, eyewear, fragrance, scarves, hats, gloves, and other products. It offers its products through retail and outlet stores, brand e-commerce sites, and concession shop-in-shops, as well as third-party digital platforms, including marketplaces. The company was formerly known as Coach, Inc. and changed its name to Tapestry, Inc. in October 2017. Tapestry, Inc. was founded in 1941 and is headquartered in New York, New York.","ceo_bio":"Ms. Joanne C. Crevoiserat | President, CEO & Director | Age 61 | Compensation: $7.3M (FY2026)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 12K people. Revenue per employee of $635K ranks in the above the sector median among Consumer Cyclical peers, with revenue growing 5%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $144K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $8K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 12\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (6/10).","financial_summary":["Tapestry, Inc. is a large-cap consumer cyclical business with a $23.6B market cap on $8.0B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +6% over the past three years and +6% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 31% against a 10% cost of capital \u2014 a 21-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat. Gross margins of 71% confirm genuine pricing power.","The business throws off $1.8B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 23 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.30\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.6\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 16\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Management returns a meaningful 8.0% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 3.0\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 8.6% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $7.7M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Consumer Cyclical tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Consumer staples outperforming discretionary, signaling cautious spending","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"High market volatility with VIX at 36, signaling elevated fear","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Widening credit spreads suggest growing stress in corporate debt markets","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Broad market in a downtrend, trading below its 200-day average","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Brand relevance decay \u2014 shifting consumer preferences and social-media-driven trends can strand inventory and invalidate positioning faster than product cycles can adapt.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 16% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Piotroski score 8/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency","Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet on this measure","16 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","Gross margin tops the Consumer Cyclical peer set \u2014 genuine pricing power is present here","Financial quality scores above most Consumer Cyclical peers","Consumer Cyclical sector showing strong relative momentum, outperforming the market by 5% over 3 months"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Wall Street Analysts See a 31.98% Upside in Tapestry (TPR): Can the Stock Really Move This High?","source":"zacks.com","link":"https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3033150/wall-street-analysts-see-a-31-98-upside-in-tapestry-tpr-can-the-stock-really-move-this-high?cid=CS-ZC-FT-fundamental_analysis%7Cconsensus_price_target-3033150","date":"2026-11-20","timestamp":1795182902.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Here's Why Tapestry (TPR) is a Strong Value Stock","source":"zacks.com","link":"https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3032409/here-s-why-tapestry-tpr-is-a-strong-value-stock?cid=CS-ZC-FT-fundamental_analysis%7Czacks_education_value_score-3032409","date":"2026-11-19","timestamp":1795095603.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentimen